   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to the provision of offsite health care services",
    "basePrintNo": "S5522",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5522-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 39,
       "fullName": "William J. Larkin Jr.",
       "imgName": "374_william_j._larkin_jr.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 374,
       "sessionMemberId": 698,
       "sessionYear": 2015,
       "shortName": "LARKIN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2803, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5522\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to the provision of\noffsite health care services\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill amends the public health law to authorize house calls.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the public health law to allow diagnostic and treatment\ncenters and outpatient clinics of general hospitals to provide house\ncalls, in certain circumstances.\n\nSection 2 - Effective Date\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill would allow house calls when a patient in need of professional\nservices available at the diagnostic and treatment center or general\nhospital outpatient clinic, is unable to go on-site due to a temporary\nor ongoing medical condition. This will enable patients to more easily\naccess health care visits and avoid the emergency department and/or\nhospital, consistent with the goals underway to transform health care\ndelivery including preventing unnecessary trips to the hospital.\n\nAdditionally, to retain and recruit physicians in rural and underserved\ncommunities, many hospitals need to employ them. However, once employed\nin the hospital outpatient department, physicians are no longer allowed\nto treat patients in the patients' homes or in nursing homes. This means\nthat elderly, infirmed, and disabled patients must be transported, many\nby ambulance, to receive routine care. This would allow diagnostic and\ntreatment centers and general hospital outpatient clinics to provide\noffsite primary care services, in certain circumstances, to patients who\nare chronically ill and temporarily or permanently homebound at their\nresidence, including those residing in long term care settings that are\nconsidered the individual's residence.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect 120 days after enactment.\n\n",
