    "lawCode": "Amd §2803, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5522A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to the provision of\noff-site health care services\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill amends the public health law to authorize house calls.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the public health law to allow diagnostic and\ntreatment centers and outpatient clinics of general hospitals to\nprovide house calls, in certain circumstances.\n\nSection 2 - Effective Date\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill would allow house calls when a patient in need of\nprofessional services available at the diagnostic and treatment center\nor general hospital outpatient clinic, is unable to go on-site due to\na temporary or ongoing medical condition. This will enable patients to\nmore easily access health care visits and avoid the emergency\ndepartment and/or hospital, consistent with the goals underway to\ntransform health care delivery including preventing unnecessary trips\nto the hospital.\n\nAdditionally, to retain and recruit physicians in rural and\nunderserved communities, many hospitals need to employ them. However,\nonce employed in the hospital outpatient department, physicians are no\nlonger allowed to treat patients in the patients' homes or in nursing\nhomes. This means that elderly, infirmed, and disabled patients must\nbe transported, many by ambulance, to receive routine care. This would\nallow diagnostic and treatment centers and general hospital outpatient\nclinics to provide offsite primary care services, in certain\ncircumstances, to patients who are chronically ill and temporarily or\npermanently homebound at their residence, including those residing in\nlong term care settings that are considered the individual's\nresidence. The bill makes clear that services provided off-site would\nbe limited to those services otherwise authorized to be provided\non-site by the diagnostic and treatment center or outpatient clinic.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect 120 days after enactment.\n\n",
